#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162856} :activity/id #uuid "c9eb15d5-cbd1-5141-92c1-c7ab44b90c6f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049750 :topic/id #uuid "10d700a2-07da-5c7f-b46c-fcc2c6f157f6" :topic/math-academy-id 3129} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163231 :tutorial/content "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix with real entries. As we know, there exists an orthonormal change of variables \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nthat transforms the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ into the quadratic form $\\mathbf{y}^TD\\mathbf{y},$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix. \n\nThe columns of the matrix $P$ are the (normalized) unit eigenvectors of $A,$ which are mutually orthogonal. These are called the **principal axes** of the quadratic form.\n\nLet's use this knowledge to find out which curve is defined by the equation \n\n$$\n6x_1^2+4x_1x_2+3x_2^2= 14,\n$$\n\nand see the geometric interpretation of the principal axes.\n\nIf we plot our curve using a graphical calculator, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8a6b6dff52e0c4004432f9447b5fd484612eaacf07588c3469af143b0a9e880.png)\n\nIt looks like an ellipse. Let's now find our coordinate system, in which the equation of the curve will be the simplest.\n\nFirst, we consider the corresponding quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})={\\color{blue}6}x_1^2+{\\color{red}4}x_1x_2+{\\color{blue}3}x_2^2,\n$$\n\nwhich we reduce to its canonical (diagonal) form. \n\nSo, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +14 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the principal axes of the curve.\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our quadratic form (and the corresponding curve) are\n\n$\\qquad$ $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that in the diagram below, the principal axes correspond to the axes $y_1$ and $y_2.$ \n\n![](images/10/10a2f0ccd179eba7f65410f932548d954a459e1bc8dcb0803a5b2d9fe7896432.png)\n\nIn the new coordinate system, our quadratic form can be reduced to \n\n$$\n7y_1^2 + 2y_2^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2} & =14 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2f9d7d81-cc50-5024-8f69-3b529968aeac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4890 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d3ecee7b-206f-5bb5-9257-61e0758f2151" :step/math-academy-id 30544 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162923}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162011 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161803 :question/id #uuid "42777bd2-44d0-5117-9e8b-ee722d8f3229" :question/math-academy-id "e-8276"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404829}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "51f3b5b0-1a5d-56cf-a16d-5c612010bfbf" :step/math-academy-id 30924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162012 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161804 :question/id #uuid "3564c452-1a51-5455-a6b1-223fda1611c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8277"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049174}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404759}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "0d0b9890-4620-5b89-b7da-8090ecf6f057" :step/math-academy-id 30925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162938}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162023 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161815 :question/id #uuid "ec194ea7-df30-5370-b0c4-d27d4b34f8c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8473"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf2e573c-f351-5d5a-8e59-9c6532f1ab9d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049456}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395188}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Curve Using a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "25baa4e8-cd30-578f-b8a2-6f5442b1bca7" :step/math-academy-id 31528 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163256 :tutorial/content "In some special cases, a quadratic equation might generate a degenerate curve or even an empty set.\n\n- For example, consider the quadratic equation \n\n$$\n-3x_1^2+8x_1x_2+3x_2^2 = 0.\n$$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n5y_1^2 - 5y_2^2 = 0\n$$\n\nwhich represents two intersecting lines $y_1 = y_2$ and $y_1 = -y_2.$\n\n- Now consider the quadratic equation \n\n$$\n-2x_1^2+8x_1x_2-8x_2^2 = 10.\n$$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n- 10y_2^2 =10\n$$\n\nwhich represents an empty set as the equation $y_2^2 = -1$ does not have any real solution.\n\n- Finally, reducing the left-hand side of \n\n$$\nx_1^2-4x_1x_2+4x_2^2 = 5\n$$\n\nto the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n5y_2^2 =5\n$$\n\nwhich represents a pair of parallel lines $y_2 = 1$ and $y_2=-1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cf07f443-3757-578e-908a-51eaef4cde16" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5077 :tutorial/title "Some Special Cases"} :step/id #uuid "2d3c6630-85aa-524d-9982-1c2a440c6b5f" :step/math-academy-id 31799 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162044 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161838 :question/id #uuid "ba46e82f-10b0-529c-94b0-3aebb7d1e1a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8678"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405181}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "ab944e55-c021-5455-a8c9-8144173eabb7" :step/math-academy-id 32221 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162900}}] :activity/title "Reducing a Quadratic Curve to Its Principal Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162224}]}
